gm-method lesson: a 'deployed site looks super out of date' report is NOT always stale CODE - witness the deploy currency FIRST, then suspect a divergent runtime CONFIG. Process that nailed it: (1) gh CI `gh run list` showed all deploys 'success' AND the deployed page carried the LATEST code marker (a this-session fix flag read live) -> code is current, NOT a CDN/cache or failed-deploy issue. CAVEAT: a LOCAL `git log origin/<deploy-branch>` ref can be stale - `git fetch` first; the branch tip was actually current (forced-update on fetch). (2) The 'out of date' LOOK was a whole feature subsystem (terrain/veg/atmosphere) not initializing: a window.* global (window.__terrain) never set, no related console logs, no pageErrors -> the init was GATED OUT, not crashing. (3) Root: the deployed singleplayer loaded a separate hand-authored WORLD CONFIG json that had DRIFTED from the canonical world module (apps/world/<name>.js) - it was missing the entire 'terrain' config block, and the backdrop init is gated on worldConfig.terrain, so it silently never ran. The standalone client config + the server-side world module are TWO sources of truth that drift; the fix is to sync the client config to the canonical world module (or generate it from one source). LESSON: when a deployed feature is absent but code is current, diff the deployed runtime CONFIG (read worldConfig live) against the canonical source - a config gate (worldConfig.terrain absent) disables a whole subsystem with zero errors. Witness the FIX end-to-end on the LIVE deployed page after the deploy run completes (fetch the branch, re-witness the window.* global is now set), not just locally - and expect the loading curtain ('Building world...') to now appear because the subsystem is finally doing its build work.